CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Management of the National Funds Act and matters necessary for its enforcement.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Decree mean the following:
1. “Enterprise special account” means a special account under Article 3 of the Government Enterprise Budget Act;
2. “Commitment to disburse” means the conclusion of contracts, etc. giving rise to disbursement under the expenditure budget, continuing expenses, acts incurring Treasury obligations and a fund management plan by a finance officer under Article 22(1) of the Management of the National Funds Act (hereinafter referred to as the “Act”) (hereinafter referred to as a “finance officer”);
3. “Commitment to pay” means an act giving rise to payment by an office operating expense cashier out of office operating expenses under Article 24(1) of the Act delivered by a disbursing officer, or by an officer authorizing transactions in the national funds investment account out of national funds;
4. “Subaccount” means one established at the Bank of Korea under the Bank of Korea Act (hereinafter referred to as the “Bank of Korea”) to record the supply and receipt of national funds under Article 86;
5. “Government and public bonds” means State bonds, bonds whose principal and interest are guaranteed by the Government, and bonds issued by local governments.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 3 (National Funds Having the Same Value as Cash) “Assets prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 2, subparagraph 1(a), of the Act means the following:
1. State deposits paid into the Bank of Korea or treasury banks (hereinafter referred to as the “Bank of Korea, etc.”) under Article 12 of the Act;
2. Treasury deposits placed with financial companies, etc. under Article 32(5);
3. State deposits placed with the Bank of Korea or treasury banks under Article 85;
4. Securities specified in the subparagraphs of Article 1(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Payment of Revenues by Securities;
5. Postal savings funds under Article 18 of the Postal Savings and Insurance Act;
6. Postal insurance reserves under Article 4 of the Postal Insurance Special Account Act;
7. Revolving funds under Article 13 of the Government Enterprise Budget Act.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 4 (Attribution of Revenues and Expenditures to Fiscal Years)
(1) Revenues under Article 4-2(2) of the Act shall be attributed to fiscal years as follows: <Amended 2015.9.15>
1. Receipts with a fixed payment period: The year in which the last day of that period falls (if the period is extended under Article 13(3), Article 5(1) of the Framework Act on National Taxes, Article 8(3) of the Customs Act, etc., meaning the last day before extension): Provided, That if a payment notice is not issued within the fiscal year in which that last day falls, they shall constitute revenues of the year in which the payment notice is issued;
2. Occasional receipts for which a payment notice is issued: The year in which the payment notice is issued;
3. Occasional receipts for which no payment notice is issued: The year in which payment is received.
(2) Expenditures under Article 4-2(2) of the Act shall be attributed to fiscal years as follows:
1. Principal and interest on State bonds and borrowings, pensions, etc.: The year in which payment falls due;
2. Various returns, amounts covering deficits, repayments, etc.: The year in which the decision to pay is made;
3. Salaries, travel expenses, fees, etc.: The year in which the fact requiring payment arises;
4. Usage charges, custody fees, electricity charges, etc.: The year to which the period of the facts giving rise to payment belongs;
5. Construction costs, manufacturing costs, consideration for purchased goods, freight charges, etc. and subsidies, etc. paid after the other party completes its act: The year in which payment is established;
6. Other expenses: The year in which a national funds deposit request or national funds transfer deposit request is issued.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

CHAPTER II DEADLINES FOR CLOSING CASH TRANSACTIONS

Article 5 (Deadline for Accepting Receipts)
(1) A cashier under Article 4-3 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “cashier”) shall accept each fiscal year's receipts by the end of that fiscal year.
(2) The Bank of Korea, etc. shall accept each fiscal year's receipts by the end of that fiscal year: Provided, That receipts may be accepted until January 20 of the following fiscal year in any of the following cases: <Amended 2015.9.15>
1. A cashier pays into the Bank of Korea, etc., under Article 12(2) of the Act, receipts accepted in that fiscal year;
2. Receipts collected as additions to local taxes are paid in;
3. Payment is made to transfer national funds between government subaccounts;
4. Amounts paid from funds for advance use under Article 18(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as “funds for advance use”) are paid in by transfer.
(3) Notwithstanding the main clause preceding the subparagraphs of paragraph (2), for receipts whose payment deadline is extended under Article 13(3), Article 5(1) of the Framework Act on National Taxes, Article 8(3) of the Customs Act, etc., the Bank of Korea, etc. may accept them until the extended deadline, being the first working day of the following fiscal year. <Newly inserted 2016.9.2>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 6 (Deadlines for Disbursement and Payment of Disbursed Funds)
(1) A disbursing officer under Article 22(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “disbursing officer”) shall disburse each fiscal year's expenses by the end of that fiscal year: Provided, That disbursement may be made until January 20 of the following fiscal year in any of the following cases: <Amended 2015.9.15>
1. Disbursement is made to transfer national funds between government subaccounts;
2. Disbursement is made to pay in, by transfer, amounts paid from funds for advance use;
3. Cash raised by issuing treasury bills or temporarily borrowing from the Bank of Korea under Article 32(1) of the Act is repaid from revenues of that fiscal year under paragraph (2) of that Article.
(2) An office operating expense cashier under Article 24(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an “office operating expense cashier”) shall pay each fiscal year's expenses by the end of that fiscal year: Provided, That charges for government purchase cards used during that fiscal year (meaning government purchase cards under Article 24(5) of the Act; hereinafter the same applies) may be paid until January 20 of the following fiscal year. <Amended 2015.9.15>
(3) The Bank of Korea, etc. shall pay each fiscal year's expenses by the end of that fiscal year: Provided, That payment may be made until January 20 of the following fiscal year in cases falling under the proviso to paragraph (1). <Amended 2015.9.15>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 7 (Deadline for Returning Disbursed Funds) Where national funds already disbursed are to be returned to the relevant disbursement item under Article 29(1) of the Act, they shall be returned by the end of the relevant fiscal year: Provided, That office operating expenses delivered to an office operating expense cashier may be returned by January 20 of the following fiscal year. <Amended 2015.9.15>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

CHAPTER III RECEIPTS

SECTION 1 COLLECTION

Article 8 (Designation of Revenue Collection Officers)
(1) When delegating affairs concerning collection of receipts under Article 9(1) of the Act, the head of a central government agency shall delegate them to the head of a subordinate agency: Provided, That where the head of the central government agency considers it unavoidable, delegation may be made to a public official other than the head of a subordinate agency.
(2) Upon designating a revenue collection officer under Article 9(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “revenue collection officer”) by delegating affairs under paragraph (1), the head of a central government agency shall notify the Minister of Finance and Economy, the Board of Audit and Inspection, and the Bank of Korea, etc. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 9 (Payment Notices) A payment notice under Article 10 of the Act shall be given to the taxpayer, etc. under the main clause of that Article (hereinafter referred to as a “taxpayer, etc.”) in a document stating the receipt item, amount payable, payment deadline, place of payment and other matters prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy (hereinafter referred to as a “payment notice”): Provided, That notice may be given orally where immediate payment to a cashier is required. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 10 (Electronic Service of Payment Notices)
(1) “Financial companies, etc. prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 11(2) of the Act means persons designated by the Minister of Finance and Economy as electronic service agencies under Article 11(3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as “electronic service agencies”) from among financial companies, etc. under Article 11(2). <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) Where a taxpayer, etc. applies for electronic service of a payment notice under Article 11(3) of the Act, the revenue collection officer shall send the payment notice to an electronic service agency using information and communications media and programs under Article 46 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the “digital budget and accounting system”), and have the agency serve it at the taxpayer's, etc. email address.
(3) Applications by taxpayers, etc. to electronic service agencies for electronic service of payment notices under Article 11(3) of the Act shall be made in a document (including an electronic document) stating the following matters: <Amended 2025.12.30>
1. The applicant's name and personal particulars, such as resident registration number;
2. Address or head office location and place of business;
3. Email address at which electronic service can be received;
4. Other matters prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy.
(4) An electronic service agency intending to discontinue electronic service shall notify the Minister of Finance and Economy and publicly announce its intention one month before the planned discontinuance date. In such cases, it shall individually notify applicants for electronic service under Article 11(3) of the Act. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(5) Where its designation is revoked or it discontinues electronic service, an electronic service agency shall delete and destroy, from its information processing devices, etc., personal information supplied by taxpayers, etc. to receive electronic service of payment notices.
(6) The head of the central government agency shall pay costs payable to electronic service agencies under Article 11(7) of the Act according to the number of notices served by each agency; matters necessary for calculating and paying the costs, etc. shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(7) Electronic service of national tax payment notices shall be governed by the Framework Act on National Taxes.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 11 (Scope of Financial Companies, etc.)
(1) “Financial companies, etc. prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the proviso to Article 12(1) of the Act means any of the following institutions: <Amended 2016.6.30>
1. Banks under Article 2(1)2 and Article 5 of the Banking Act (hereinafter referred to as “banks”);
2. Postal agencies;
3. The National Credit Union Federation of Korea under the Credit Unions Act;
4. The Korean Federation of Community Credit Cooperatives under the Community Credit Cooperatives Act;
5. The Korea Federation of Savings Banks under the Mutual Savings Banks Act;
6. The National Forestry Cooperatives Federation under the Forestry Cooperatives Act;
7. Financial investment business entities under Article 8(1) of the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act.
(2) “Financial companies, etc. prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 18(3) of the Act means the institutions under paragraph (1), the Bank of Korea, national tax payment agencies under Article 12(1)3 of the National Tax Collection Act, and payment agencies for national funds other than national taxes (hereinafter referred to as “financial companies, etc.”). <Amended 2021.2.17>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 12 (Provision of Information on Payment Notices) The head of a central government agency shall provide the Minister of Finance and Economy, through the digital budget and accounting system under Article 17 of the Act, with the following information concerning payment notices under that agency's jurisdiction: <Amended 2013.6.28, 2025.12.30>
1. Receipt item;
2. Amount payable;
3. Payment deadline;
4. Name of the taxpayer, etc. and real-name identification number (meaning a resident registration number, business registration number or unique identification number under Article 12(2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Value-Added Tax Act);
5. Payment notice number;
6. Revenue collection officer's account number;
7. Other matters prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 13 (Notification of Payment Deadlines, etc.)
(1) When giving a payment notice under Articles 9 and 10, the revenue collection officer shall set and notify a deadline within 15 days from the notice date, except where relevant statutes or regulations, the relevant contract, etc. fix the deadline: Provided, That this does not apply to the proviso to Article 9.
(2) When giving a notice for receipts whose payment deadline is fixed under relevant statutes or regulations, the relevant contract, etc., the revenue collection officer shall issue and send the payment notice to the taxpayer, etc. five days before the payment period begins.
(3) Where the payment deadline under paragraph (1) falls on a public holiday, Saturday or Workers' Day under the Designation of Workers' Day Act, the following day shall be the deadline.
(4) For electronic payment of receipts through information and communications media, where an outage of the digital budget and accounting system during the payment period under paragraph (1), due to a power failure, error in information and communications media or programs, or another unavoidable reason, prevents electronic payment, the deadline shall be the day after the outage is repaired and electronic payment becomes possible.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

SECTION 2 ACCEPTANCE OF RECEIPTS

Article 14 (Procedures for Acceptance by Revenue Cashiers)
(1) A revenue cashier under the main clause of Article 12(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “revenue cashier”) who accepts receipts shall issue a receipt to the payer and notify the revenue collection officer of the details of acceptance, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) Notwithstanding Article 12(2) of the Act, where receipts are accepted abroad, the head of a central government agency may deduct those receipts from office operating expenses to be delivered to the receiving institution, and have the office operating expense cashier delivering those expenses pay the deducted amount into the Bank of Korea, etc.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 15 (Procedures for Acceptance by the Bank of Korea, etc.)
(1) The Bank of Korea, etc. (including financial companies, etc. handling transactions in national funds on their behalf under Article 36(2) of the Act; hereinafter the same applies in this Article) shall, when accepting receipts, issue a receipt to the payer and notify the revenue collection officer of the details of acceptance, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy: Provided, That no receipt shall be issued where the payer makes electronic payment through information and communications media. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) Where the deadline stated in a payment notice or under other statutes or regulations falls on a non-business day of the Bank of Korea, etc., the payer may pay the receipts by the following day.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 16 (Concurrent Accounting Duties) Where an unavoidably small authorized staff complement requires one person to perform several duties concurrently, the duties of revenue collection officer and revenue cashier, and the duties of disbursing officer and cashier, may respectively be performed by the same person under the provisos to Articles 13 and 27 of the Act.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

SECTION 3 RETURN OF ERRONEOUS PAYMENTS AND OVERPAYMENTS, ETC.

Article 17 (Return of Erroneous Payments and Overpayments, etc.)
(1) Where a revenue collection officer intends to return erroneous payments or overpayments under Article 15 of the Act (including interest thereon; hereinafter the same applies), the officer shall confirm the receipt item, receipt year, amount, etc. of the erroneous payment or overpayment, decide upon its return, and notify the payer of the result without delay.
(2) Upon deciding to return an erroneous payment or overpayment under paragraph (1), the revenue collection officer shall request the Bank of Korea, etc. to pay the return amount.
(3) Article 28 applies mutatis mutandis to the method and procedures, etc. for returning erroneous payments and overpayments. In such cases, “disbursing officer” is construed as “revenue collection officer”.
(4) Paragraphs (1) through (3) apply mutatis mutandis where a revenue collection officer refunds receipts under Article 16 of the Act.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 17-2 (Interest on Erroneous Payments and Overpayments) Interest under the main clause of Article 15(2) of the Act shall be calculated at the interest rate under Article 43-3(2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on National Taxes for the period from the day after the date specified below through the date of return:
1. Return following confirmation, through an objection or otherwise, of an erroneous payment, duplicate payment or other incorrect payment: The date the erroneous payment or overpayment was made: Provided, That if payment was made in two or more installments, the date of the last payment applies; if the return exceeds that last payment, the respective dates of payment, counted backwards in payment order until the return amount is reached, apply;
2. Return following amendment of relevant statutes or regulations after lawful payment: The enforcement date of those statutes or regulations;
3. Return following reduction or exemption of the amount after lawful payment: The date of the decision on reduction or exemption;
4. Return after lawful payment because of a change in a project plan, etc., cancellation of a permit, etc. for reasons attributable to the payer, or other comparable reasons: The date of the decision on the relevant administrative disposition.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 18 (Notification of Return of Erroneous Payments and Overpayments by the Bank of Korea, etc.) Upon returning an erroneous payment or overpayment or refunding receipts under Article 17, the Bank of Korea, etc. shall account for the result and notify the relevant revenue collection officer of the return or refund.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 18-2 (Procedures for Payment of Local Consumption Tax Collected by the State)
(1) A local consumption tax account shall be established at the Bank of Korea to efficiently manage acceptance and payment of local consumption tax under Article 16-2 of the Act.
(2) Upon collecting local consumption tax, a revenue collection officer shall request the Bank of Korea to transfer it to the local consumption tax account by the 15th of the following month, and submit the collection details prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy to the Commissioner of the National Tax Service or the Commissioner of the Korea Customs Service. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(3) Where a revenue collection officer of a subordinate agency collects local consumption tax and requests the Bank of Korea to transfer it to the local consumption tax account under paragraph (2), the Commissioner of the Korea Customs Service shall inform the Commissioner of the National Tax Service of the collection details.
(4) The Commissioner of the National Tax Service shall check the collection details submitted by revenue collection officers of subordinate agencies (including details received from the Commissioner of the Korea Customs Service under paragraph (3)), determine the amount of local consumption tax to be paid, and request the Bank of Korea to pay that amount to the local consumption tax payment administrator under Article 71 of the Local Tax Act.
(5) Where a revenue collection officer of a subordinate agency refunds more local consumption tax than was collected, the Commissioner of the National Tax Service shall request the Bank of Korea to transfer the excess amount from the local consumption tax account to that revenue collection officer's account.
(6) A revenue collection officer or the Commissioner of the National Tax Service requesting transfer or payment from the Bank of Korea under paragraph (2), (4) or (5) shall state the matters prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(7) Upon receiving a transfer or payment request under paragraph (2), (4) or (5), the Bank of Korea shall take necessary measures without delay and notify the revenue collection officer and the Commissioner of the National Tax Service of the result. In such cases, the payment deadlines for disbursed funds under Article 6 do not apply.
(8) To perform cash transfer and payment affairs for the local consumption tax account under paragraph (1), the Commissioner of the National Tax Service shall appoint an officer authorizing transactions in that account and a cashier for that account from among subordinate public officials: Provided, That appointment may be replaced by designation of positions established in a subordinate agency.
(9) Upon appointing an officer authorizing transactions in the local consumption tax account, the Commissioner of the National Tax Service shall notify the Board of Audit and Inspection; upon appointing a cashier for that account, the Commissioner shall notify both the Board of Audit and Inspection and the Bank of Korea.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

CHAPTER IV OPERATION OF FUNDS FOR ADVANCE USE

Article 19 (Appointment of Officers Authorizing Transactions in Funds for Advance Use and Cashiers for Those Funds) Appointment of officers authorizing transactions in funds for advance use and cashiers for those funds under Article 18(2) of the Act may be replaced by designation of positions established in the relevant agency.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 20 (Request for Delivery of Funds for Advance Use)
(1) The head of a central government agency responsible for an enterprise special account may request the Bank of Korea, etc. to deliver the cash needed for disbursement from funds for advance use to the cashier for funds for advance use under Article 18(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “cashier for funds for advance use”). The requested amount may not exceed the disbursement ceiling for funds for advance use under Article 21(1).
(2) Cash delivered under paragraph (1) shall be returned within the relevant fiscal year.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 21 (Notification of Disbursement Ceilings for Funds for Advance Use)
(1) Where the head of a central government agency intends to operate funds for advance use for an enterprise special account under that agency's jurisdiction, the head shall notify the officer authorizing transactions in funds for advance use under Article 18(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an “officer authorizing transactions in funds for advance use”) of the disbursement ceiling for those funds.
(2) The ceiling under paragraph (1) may not exceed the expenditure budget allocated under Article 43 of the National Finance Act.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 22 (Procedures for Disbursement from Funds for Advance Use)
(1) A finance officer intending to disburse from funds for advance use pursuant to a commitment to disburse shall forward the documents concerning the commitment to the officer authorizing transactions in funds for advance use.
(2) Upon receiving commitment documents from the finance officer, the officer authorizing transactions in funds for advance use shall verify that the commitment falls within the allocated budget and the disbursement ceiling for those funds and is appropriate for the budget item of the relevant fiscal year, and then request the cashier for those funds to disburse.
(3) The cashier for funds for advance use shall disburse from those funds in accordance with the request of the officer authorizing transactions in those funds.
(4) When intending to disburse from funds for advance use to a creditor, etc. under Article 28(1), or deliver them to an office operating expense cashier for payment, the cashier for funds for advance use shall disburse or deliver by account transfer.
(5) Except as otherwise provided in this Decree, provisions concerning revenue cashiers and office operating expense cashiers apply mutatis mutandis to cashiers for funds for advance use.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 23 (Notification of Cash Transactions in Funds for Advance Use and Settlement by Transfer)
(1) By the 10th of the following month, a cashier for funds for advance use shall notify the disbursing officer of the status of transactions in those funds as at the end of each month.
(2) The disbursing officer shall pay the amount disbursed from funds for advance use to the revenue collection officer by the 15th of the following month, in accordance with the status notified under paragraph (1).
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

CHAPTER V DISBURSEMENTS

SECTION 1 COMMITMENTS TO DISBURSE

Article 24 (Delegation of Commitments to Disburse) Upon delegating affairs concerning commitments to disburse to subordinate public officials under Article 21 of the Act, the head of a central government agency shall notify the disbursing officer, the Minister of Finance and Economy and the Board of Audit and Inspection. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 25 (Restrictions on Commitments to Disburse)
(1) A finance officer delegated affairs concerning commitments to disburse under Article 21 of the Act may not make commitments exceeding the budget reallocated under Article 43-2 of the National Finance Act. <Amended 2022.3.22>
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), a finance officer intending to execute expenses financed by receipts in excess of the budget under Article 53(1) of the National Finance Act may make commitments to disburse within both the ceiling for execution in excess of the budget for such expenses notified to each finance officer under Article 24 of the Enforcement Decree of the National Finance Act and the receipts exceeding the budget.
(3) The head of a central government agency responsible for a fund may establish a monthly cash operation plan for that fund within its fund management plan; where notified of the monthly cash operation plan by that head, the fund's finance officer shall make commitments to disburse within that monthly plan.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

SECTION 2 METHODS OF DISBURSEMENT

Article 26 (Appointment of Disbursing Officers)
(1) The head of a central government agency shall appoint disbursing officers under Article 22 of the Act to make disbursements pursuant to commitments to disburse by finance officers under that agency's jurisdiction.
(2) Upon appointing a disbursing officer under paragraph (1), the head of a central government agency shall notify the finance officer, the Minister of Finance and Economy, the Board of Audit and Inspection, and the Bank of Korea, etc. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 27 (Standards for Disbursement) A disbursing officer intending to disburse under Article 22 of the Act shall verify that the following requirements are satisfied:
1. The commitment to disburse was made under Article 20 of the Act and Article 25 of this Decree;
2. Documents concerning the commitment to disburse were forwarded under Article 22 of the Act;
3. The amount is within each budget item in the detailed monthly cash plan for each disbursing officer under Article 50;
4. The commitment is recorded in the commitment-to-disburse ledger under Article 92(2);
5. For a fund, the amount is within its holdings excluding surplus cash currently invested (including cash to be received or recovered before the scheduled disbursement date).
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 28 (Methods of Disbursement)
(1) A disbursing officer intending to disburse under Article 22(3) of the Act shall have the Bank of Korea, etc. pay by transfer to the deposit account, at a bank or other institution performing financial business (hereinafter referred to as a “bank, etc.”), of the creditor or a person entrusted under statutes or regulations with handling payment of national funds (hereinafter referred to as a “creditor, etc.”).
(2) When having the Bank of Korea, etc. make a transfer to the deposit account of a creditor, etc. under paragraph (1), the disbursing officer shall send it a national funds deposit request or national funds transfer deposit request prescribed by the Minister of Finance and Economy (the latter limited to transfers of national funds between government subaccounts; hereinafter the same applies). <Amended 2025.12.30>
(3) Where an outage of information and communications media and programs at the bank, etc. holding a creditor's, etc. deposit account prevents transfer, the disbursing officer may, with the creditor's, etc. cooperation, transfer to a deposit account at a bank, etc. capable of receiving an account transfer.
(4) Matters necessary for transfer methods, procedures, etc. for transfers to creditors', etc. accounts under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 29 (Processing Disbursements through the National Financial Information System)
(1) A disbursing officer making disbursements under Articles 27 and 28 shall perform the affairs through the digital budget and accounting system.
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), where an outage of information and communications media and programs, etc. prevents use of the digital budget and accounting system to send a national funds deposit request or national funds transfer deposit request under Article 28(2) (hereinafter referred to as a “national funds deposit request, etc.”) to the Bank of Korea, etc., the disbursing officer may print the request, etc. and send it by a method prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 30 (Disbursement by Methods Other than Account Transfer)
(1) Where an unavoidable reason under Article 22(4) of the Act causes an outage of information and communications media and programs at all banks, etc., making account-transfer payment under Article 22(3) of the Act impossible, the Minister of Finance and Economy may have disbursement made by direct payment through treasury checks or another method, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) When disbursements are made by the method under paragraph (1), the Minister of Finance and Economy shall immediately notify the heads of central government agencies, the Bank of Korea, etc. and banks, etc., respectively, and publicly announce the fact. The same applies when the unavoidable reason under Article 22(4) of the Act is resolved and disbursement by treasury checks, etc. under paragraph (1) is discontinued. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

SECTION 3 SPECIAL CASES FOR DISBURSEMENT

Article 31 (Scope of Office Operating Expenses) The scope of office operating expenses under Article 24(6) of the Act is as follows: <Amended 2017.12.29, 2023.1.17, 2025.12.30>
1. Expenses not exceeding the amount prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy, among operating expenses (excluding employee welfare expenses, school operating expenses, general service expenses and management service expenses), special activity expenses, national security expenses, information security expenses and business promotion expenses;
2. Expenses paid where a creditor abroad wishes to receive payment abroad (including expenses paid to overseas diplomatic missions and State agencies established abroad);
3. Travel expenses;
4. Other expenses prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy whose disbursement through the procedures in Articles 28 through 30 is liable to impede the performance of duties.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 32 (Delivery of Office Operating Expenses and Opening of Treasury Deposits)
(1) A disbursing officer delivering office operating expenses shall deliver them each month on the basis of the amount expected to be paid by the office operating expense cashier.
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), where the head of the central government agency considers it necessary for cash transactions, the disbursing officer may deliver office operating expenses for up to three months.
(3) A disbursing officer shall deliver office operating expenses by a national funds deposit request under Article 28(2), setting a spending ceiling for each budget item.
(4) An office operating expense cashier may deliver the expenses in the subparagraphs of Article 31 to military units of regimental level or below for use.
(5) An office operating expense cashier shall deposit and manage funds delivered under paragraph (1) in deposits opened at financial companies, etc. (hereinafter referred to as “treasury deposits”), as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(6) Treasury deposits shall be opened in a manner enabling notification of their transaction status to the Bank of Korea through a transmission system.
(7) Matters necessary for methods of opening treasury deposits, payment of interest, operation, etc. shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 33 (Restrictions on Payment of Office Operating Expenses) An office operating expense cashier shall pay office operating expenses within the spending ceiling for each budget item under Article 32(3).
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 33-2 (Commitments to Pay and Payment Resolutions)
(1) An office operating expense cashier may make commitments to pay within the spending ceilings by budget item delivered by the disbursing officer. The same applies when adopting a payment resolution pursuant to a commitment to pay.
(2) A commitment to pay under paragraph (1) may not contravene the purpose for which the funds were delivered.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 34 (Use of Government Purchase Cards)
(1) An office operating expense cashier may obtain several government purchase cards and issue them to public officials of the agency for use. In such cases, public officials receiving and using the cards are deemed office operating expense cashiers.
(2) Government purchase cards may not be used for any purpose other than paying office operating expenses under Article 24(5) of the Act.
(3) Matters necessary for issuance and use, etc. of government purchase cards shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 35 (Conclusion of Agreements for Use of Government Purchase Cards) A person intending to issue and operate government purchase cards under Article 24(5) of the Act and Article 34 of this Decree shall be any of the following persons, satisfy technical requirements prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy, and conclude an agreement with the office operating expense cashier concerning use of government purchase cards: <Amended 2017.12.29, 2025.12.30>
1. A person operating a credit card business under Article 2, subparagraph 2, of the Specialized Credit Finance Business Act;
2. A person operating electronic financial business under Article 2, subparagraph 1, of the Electronic Financial Transactions Act.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 36 (Payment of Office Operating Expenses in Cash, etc.)
(1) Where an office operating expense cashier cannot pay office operating expenses using a government purchase card, payment shall be made by account transfer (including automatic transfers of utility charges, etc.; hereinafter the same applies): Provided, That cash payment is permitted in the following cases: <Amended 2025.12.30>
1. Payment of expenses under Article 31, subparagraphs 1 and 4, for purposes prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy;
2. Payment of expenses under Article 31, subparagraph 2;
3. Among expenses under Article 31, subparagraph 3, domestic travel expenses and overseas travel expenses paid to foreigners;
4. Where government purchase cards cannot be used because no credit card merchant exists, etc. in the area where expenses are to be used in the agency's locality, such as an island, remote place or remote mountain area;
5. Redelivery of office operating expenses to a divided-duty office operating expense cashier under Article 105(2).
(2) Notwithstanding the proviso preceding the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), where the creditor requests account transfer, payment shall be made by account transfer.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 37 (Return of Unused Office Operating Expenses)
(1) An office operating expense cashier shall return the unused balance of office operating expenses for each fiscal year to the relevant disbursing officer by January 20 of the following fiscal year. Article 14(2) may apply mutatis mutandis to the method of returning unused office operating expenses of overseas diplomatic missions (including State agencies established abroad; hereinafter the same applies). <Amended 2015.9.15>
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), where an office operating expense cashier receives funds from an officer authorizing transactions in funds for advance use, the cashier shall return the unused balance of office operating expenses for each fiscal year to that authorizing officer by January 20 of the following fiscal year. <Amended 2015.9.15>
(3) For overseas diplomatic missions and officials stationed abroad, deduction of the unused balance of office operating expenses for each fiscal year from office operating expenses to be delivered to those missions and officials for the following fiscal year may replace return.
(4) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), an office operating expense cashier may carry forward and use unused office operating expenses in the following year in the following cases:
1. Amounts for which a commitment to pay has been made but payment has not been made;
2. Charges for government purchase cards used in the immediately preceding fiscal year that remain unpaid;
3. Facility expenses of overseas diplomatic missions for which a commitment to pay has been made but payment has not been made.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 38 (Delivery of Office Operating Expenses before the Start of a Fiscal Year)
(1) Expenses for which funds may be delivered to office operating expense cashiers before the start of a fiscal year under Article 25(3) of the Act are as follows: <Amended 2017.12.29, 2023.1.17>
1. Operating expenses (excluding employee welfare expenses, school operating expenses, general service expenses and management service expenses);
2. Business promotion expenses, special activity expenses, national security expenses and information security expenses;
3. Expenses paid abroad;
4. Domestic travel expenses.
(2) Where the head of a central government agency intends to deliver funds before the fiscal year begins under paragraph (1), the head shall prepare and submit a statement of the amounts to be delivered to the Minister of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 39 (Use of Office Operating Expenses by Overseas Diplomatic Missions)
(1) Notwithstanding Article 32(5), an overseas diplomatic mission may deposit and manage office operating expenses with banks, etc. or foreign banks, etc. in its locality.
(2) An office operating expense cashier of an overseas diplomatic mission intending to pay office operating expenses may use a credit card (including credit cards issued by foreign credit card businesses), or pay by account transfer or in cash, by applying Article 24(5) of the Act and Article 36 of this Decree mutatis mutandis.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 40 (Advance Payments)
(1) Expenses that may be paid in advance under Article 26 of the Act are as follows: <Amended 2014.8.6, 2020.4.21, 2024.2.13, 2025.12.30>
1. Prices of machinery, books, specimens or experimental materials purchased directly abroad;
2. Expenses for purchasing periodicals;
3. Land or building rent and vessel charter hire;
4. Freight charges;
5. Pay to enlisted soldiers transferred on the salary reference date, or on a business trip, emergency deployment, mobile training or leave;
6. Expenses paid to State agencies, local governments and public institutions under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions (hereinafter referred to as “State agencies, etc.”);
7. Expenses paid to persons engaged in research or investigation abroad;
8. Pay to persons serving in places with inconvenient transportation or to crew members of vessels;
9. National funds where affairs concerning their payment are entrusted to banks, etc.;
10. Expenses necessary for entrustment of affairs, etc. (including cases under subparagraph 9);
11. Subsidies, contributions and grants;
12. Honoraria;
13. Expenses paid to United Nations organizations;
14. Prices, compensation or relocation expenses for land purchased or expropriated by the State or objects situated on that land;
15. An amount not exceeding 70 percent of the contract price for construction, manufacturing or service contracts (100 percent where the head of a central government agency has consulted the Minister of Finance and Economy because it is necessary for smooth construction progress, etc.);
16. Expenses paid in Korea to foreigners invited by the Government;
17. Prices of procurement supplies under Article 2, subparagraph 1, of the Public Procurement Service Act paid to the Public Procurement Service;
18. Expenses for disaster relief and recovery.
(2) Where payment of public officials' pay is entrusted to banks, etc. under paragraph (1)9, the relevant amount may be deposited in advance with the entrusted bank, etc. within three days before payday.
(3) Expenses under paragraph (1)15, if paid in advance, shall be paid within 14 days from receipt of the other contracting party's request after conclusion of the contract: Provided, That this does not apply where unavoidable reasons prevent payment and those reasons are notified to the other contracting party in writing.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 41 (Payments on an Estimated Basis) Expenses that may be paid on an estimated basis under Article 26 of the Act are as follows: <Amended 2025.12.30>
1. Expenses paid to State agencies, etc.;
2. Subsidies, contributions and grants;
3. Expenses paid to United Nations organizations;
4. Expenses for disaster relief and recovery;
5. Other expenses prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 42 (Expenses Disbursable in Excess of Unused Amounts) Expenses consisting of established debts attributable to a previous year that may be disbursed in excess of unused amounts under the proviso to Article 28 of the Act are as follows:
1. Public officials' remuneration;
2. Death compensation;
3. Disability compensation;
4. Insurance premiums;
5. Damages and compensation;
6. Returns, deficit compensation and repayments;
7. Insurance proceeds and returned insurance premiums;
8. Interest;
9. Costs under international treaties and agreements;
10. Compensation for the price of farmland;
11. Infectious disease prevention and quarantine expenses;
12. Expenses for accommodating patients.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 43 (Entrustment of Expense Payment Affairs) Where necessary for efficient payment of expenses, the head of a central government agency may entrust the Bank of Korea, etc. or banks, etc. with making payment.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 44 (Maintenance of Books for Entrusted Expenses, etc.)
(1) The Bank of Korea, etc. or banks, etc. entrusted with expense payment affairs under Article 43 shall maintain books concerning the receipt and payment of the expenses delivered to them (hereinafter referred to in this Article as “entrusted expenses”) and record necessary matters.
(2) The Bank of Korea, etc. or banks, etc. entrusted under Article 43 shall prepare a report on transactions in entrusted expenses for each fiscal year and submit it to the head of the central government agency entrusting payment by January 31 of the following fiscal year.
(3) The Bank of Korea, etc. or banks, etc. shall submit a statement of accounts of transactions in entrusted expenses for each fiscal year to the head of the central government agency entrusting the funds and the Board of Audit and Inspection by January 31 of the following fiscal year.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

SECTION 4 RETURN OF DISBURSED FUNDS

Article 45 (Return of Disbursed Funds) A disbursing officer intending to receive a return of an amount disbursed under Article 29(1) of the Act shall issue a return notice to the person obliged to return it. The payment notice method under Article 9 applies mutatis mutandis to such return notices.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 46 (Procedures for Acceptance of Returned Amounts by the Bank of Korea, etc.) Upon accepting a returned amount under Article 45, the Bank of Korea, etc. shall issue a receipt to the person returning the disbursed funds and notify the disbursing officer of the details of acceptance, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy: Provided, That where return is made after the last day of the fiscal year in which disbursement was made, the revenue collection officer shall be notified. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

CHAPTER VI CASH MANAGEMENT

SECTION 1 CASH PLANS

Article 47 (Preparation of Monthly Cash Plans for Each Central Government Agency) Monthly cash plans submitted by the heads of central government agencies to the Minister of Finance and Economy under Article 30 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as “monthly cash plans by central government agency”) shall clearly state the following: <Amended 2025.12.30>
1. Receipt plan: Classification by division, article and item prescribed in the revenue budget;
2. Disbursement plan: Classification by chapter, division, article and subarticle prescribed in the expenditure budget.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 48 (Adjustment and Modification of Monthly Cash Plans)
(1) When preparing a monthly cash plan under Article 30(2) of the Act, the Minister of Finance and Economy may adjust the contents of monthly cash plans by central government agency where necessary for cash supply and demand. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) A monthly cash plan under paragraph (1) shall be classified by jurisdiction and presented in comparison with the amounts submitted by the heads of central government agencies.
(3) Where unavoidable reasons require modification of the disbursement plan within a monthly cash plan prepared by the Minister of Finance and Economy under Article 30(2) of the Act, the head of a central government agency shall submit a request for modification of the monthly cash plan to that Minister. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(4) Where a request for modification under paragraph (3) is made or an unavoidable reason concerning cash supply and demand arises, the Minister of Finance and Economy may modify the monthly cash plan. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(5) Upon modifying a monthly cash plan under paragraph (4), the Minister of Finance and Economy shall notify the head of the relevant central government agency, the Minister of Planning and Budget and the Bank of Korea. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 49 (Preparation and Modification of Detailed Monthly Cash Plans)
(1) The head of a central government agency shall prepare and submit a detailed monthly cash plan under Article 30(3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “detailed monthly cash plan by central government agency”) to the Minister of Finance and Economy no later than one week before the last working day of each month. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) Detailed monthly cash plans by central government agency shall be prepared in five-day units in accordance with the item classifications in monthly cash plans by central government agency, clearly stating:
1. Date cash is required, amount and reason;
2. Other matters necessary to forecast cash requirements.
(3) The Minister of Finance and Economy shall compile the detailed monthly cash plans by central government agency under paragraphs (1) and (2), prepare the Government's detailed monthly cash plan, and notify the heads of central government agencies and the Bank of Korea thereof. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(4) When preparing and notifying a detailed monthly cash plan under paragraph (3), the Minister of Finance and Economy may adjust the item classifications in the notification. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(5) Where unavoidable reasons concerning cash transactions require modification of the detailed monthly cash plan notified by the Minister of Finance and Economy under Article 30(4) of the Act, the head of a central government agency shall prepare and submit a request for modification of the detailed monthly cash plan to that Minister. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(6) Where a request for modification under paragraph (5) is made, or where necessary for cash supply and demand, the Minister of Finance and Economy may modify the detailed monthly cash plan. In such cases, the Minister of Finance and Economy shall notify the heads of central government agencies and the Bank of Korea of the modified detailed monthly cash plan. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(7) Upon notifying each finance officer of the ceiling for execution of expenses financed by receipts in excess of the budget under Article 24(3) of the Enforcement Decree of the National Finance Act, the head of a central government agency shall reflect it in the detailed monthly cash plan.
(8) Detailed monthly cash plans by central government agency, detailed monthly cash plans, requests for modification of detailed monthly cash plans and detailed monthly cash plans by disbursing officer under paragraphs (1) through (7), Article 48(3) and Article 50(1) shall be prepared, submitted and notified using the digital budget and accounting system.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 50 (Preparation and Modification of Detailed Monthly Cash Plans by Disbursing Officer)
(1) Within the detailed monthly cash plan notified by the Minister of Finance and Economy under Article 49(3), the head of a central government agency shall prepare a detailed monthly cash plan for each subordinate disbursing officer and notify the relevant officer. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) Where necessary for cash transactions, the head of a central government agency may modify the detailed monthly cash plans by disbursing officer notified under paragraph (1).
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

SECTION 1-2 OPERATION OF THE INTEGRATED ACCOUNT <Newly inserted 2005.6.30>

Article 50-2 (Operation and Management of the Integrated Account)
(1) The Minister of Finance and Economy shall manage cash of the general account and special accounts on an integrated basis through the integrated account under Article 31(1) and (2) of the Act: Provided, That cash of the following special accounts is excluded: <Amended 2025.12.30>
1. The Postal Insurance Special Account under the Postal Insurance Special Account Act;
2. The Postal Savings Special Account and the Grain Management Special Account among enterprise special accounts.
(2) The integrated account under Article 31(2) of the Act shall include amounts carried over from the previous year and surplus revenues of accounts or subaccounts subject to integration (including the Treasury receipts settlement account).
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

SECTION 2 RAISING CASH

Article 51 (Raising Cash)
(1) Where necessary for the cash supply and demand of accounts and subaccounts, or upon receiving a request under paragraph (2), the Minister of Finance and Economy may raise necessary cash through mutual deposits of national funds, issuance of treasury bills or temporary borrowing from the Bank of Korea. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) The head of a central government agency responsible for a special account or subaccount who wishes to obtain cash to cover a temporary shortfall shall request the Minister of Finance and Economy to raise cash, clearly stating the following: <Amended 2025.12.30>
1. Reason cash is needed;
2. Amount needed;
3. Year, month and day on which cash must be raised;
4. Repayment deadline and plan;
5. Other reference matters.
(3) In making mutual deposits of national funds under Article 31(3) of the Act, the Minister of Finance and Economy may pay interest on that cash within the budget. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 52 (Payment of Principal and Interest on Cash Raised)
(1) The head of a central government agency responsible for an account or subaccount using cash temporarily borrowed from the Bank of Korea under Article 32(4) of the Act and Article 51(1) and (2) of this Decree shall pay the principal and interest to the Bank of Korea by the repayment date.
(2) The head of a central government agency responsible for an account or subaccount using cash raised by issuing treasury bills under Article 33(1) of the Act and Article 51(1) and (2) of this Decree shall pay the issuance expenses to a person designated by the Minister of Finance and Economy by the day before the bills' issuance date, and pay the principal and interest due to the Bank of Korea by the day before their redemption date. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

SECTION 3 ISSUANCE OF TREASURY BILLS

Article 53 (Methods of Issuing Treasury Bills)
(1) Treasury bills issued on the open market under Article 33(2) of the Act shall be issued by subscription, sale or competitive bidding.
(2) Where necessary, treasury bills may be sold under the proviso to Article 33(2) of the Act to financial companies, etc., government-invested enterprises and insurance companies, and to any of the following persons: <Amended 2025.12.30>
1. Investment traders, investment brokers, securities finance companies and merchant banks under the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act;
2. Other persons determined by the Minister of Finance and Economy.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 54 (Methods of Public Announcement) The Minister of Finance and Economy shall make public announcements under Article 33(3) of the Act through the Official Gazette, newspapers, information and communications media, etc.: Provided, That for sales of treasury bills under Article 53(2), public announcement may be replaced by direct notification through the relevant institutions or their federations. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 55 (Issuance of Treasury Bills through Entrustment or Underwriting)
(1) When issuing treasury bills by subscription or sale, the Minister of Finance and Economy may entrust issuance to or have the bills underwritten by persons designated by that Minister. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) Upon issuing treasury bills under paragraph (1), the Minister of Finance and Economy may pay a fee not exceeding 10/1,000 of the sale proceeds. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 56 (Adjustment of the Amount of Treasury Bills Issued) Where treasury bills are issued by subscription or bidding and the total subscribed amount or the total amount bid at or below the discount rate provisionally fixed by the Minister of Finance and Economy (hereinafter referred to as the “total amount of eligible bids”) falls short of or exceeds the issue amount, the Minister of Finance and Economy may adjust the issue amount within the total subscribed amount or total amount of eligible bids. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 57 (Form of Treasury Bills)
(1) The Minister of Finance and Economy shall determine the types, specifications and appearance of treasury bills: Provided, That specifications and appearance are relevant only where treasury bills are issued. <Amended 2016.9.2, 2025.12.30>
(2) Treasury bills shall bear symbols and numbers.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 58 (Exchange, Division and Consolidation of Treasury Bills) A holder of treasury bills issued under the proviso to Article 33(4) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “treasury bill holder”) may request the Bank of Korea to exchange, divide or consolidate those bills: Provided, That this applies only to bills with identical issuance dates, redemption deadlines and other issuance terms. <Amended 2016.9.2>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 59 (Registration of Treasury Bills)
(1) In performing registration affairs under Article 33(9) of the Act, the Bank of Korea shall maintain the computerized information processing system and electronic treasury bill register under the main clause of paragraph (4) of that Article (hereinafter referred to as the “electronic treasury bill register”).
(2) Upon application by a creditor, the Bank of Korea shall register the following matters in the electronic treasury bill register:
1. Name and address of the creditor (limited to creditors under registered treasury bills);
2. Registered amount (based on face value);
3. Issuance series and issuance date of the treasury bills;
4. Maturity date of the treasury bills;
5. Other matters necessary for registration, such as management numbers.
[Wholly amended 2016.9.2]

Article 60 (Exceptional Issuance of Certificates)
(1) When issuing certificates under the proviso to Article 33(4) of the Act, the Minister of Finance and Economy shall state on the treasury bills the matters in the subparagraphs of Article 4(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the State Bond Act. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) A treasury bill holder registering certificates shall submit an application for registration of treasury bills to the Bank of Korea, attaching the bills to be registered.
[Wholly amended 2016.9.2]

Article 61 (Changes to Registration)
(1) The registered holder of treasury bills registered under the main clause of Article 33(4) of the Act and Article 60(2) of this Decree (hereinafter referred to as “registered treasury bills”) shall submit an application for change of treasury bill registration to the Bank of Korea to change registered matters. <Amended 2016.9.2>
(2) Where registered matters are to be changed because of a transfer of rights, both parties shall affix their names and seals to the application for change of registration.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 62 (Cancellation of Registration) When redeeming registered treasury bills, the Bank of Korea shall cancel their registration upon the registered holder's application for redemption of the registered treasury bills.
[Wholly amended 2016.9.2]

Article 63 (Registration of Pledges)
(1) To register the creation of a pledge or a subpledge over registered treasury bills, an application for pledge registration bearing both parties' names and seals shall be submitted to the Bank of Korea.
(2) Paragraph (1) applies mutatis mutandis to changes or cancellation of registered matters concerning a pledge: Provided, That cancellation may be made at the request of either party where documentary evidence of the reason for cancellation is attached or the pledgee applies for cancellation.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 64 (Registration Notices) Upon making a new registration, registration of a change or registration of a pledge in the electronic treasury bill register, the Bank of Korea shall issue a registration notice to the registered holder of the registered treasury bills or the applicant. <Amended 2016.9.2>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 65 (Restrictions on Registered Amounts) Amounts of new registrations, changes to registration, registrations of pledges and cancellations of registration of treasury bills shall be divisible by the minimum denomination of treasury bills.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 66 (Inspection of the Register, etc.) A registered holder of registered treasury bills may apply to the Bank of Korea to inspect the electronic treasury bill register or obtain a certificate of the current registered amount of treasury bills. <Amended 2016.9.2>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 67 (Submission of Registered Seals) Registered holders of registered treasury bills and persons intending to exercise rights thereunder shall submit their registered seals under the Certification of Seal Imprint Act to the Bank of Korea.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 68 (Reporting Appointment of Agents, etc.) Where a registered holder of registered treasury bills appoints an agent, the holder shall report the fact to the Bank of Korea. The same applies when the agent is changed or dismissed.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 69 (Notices concerning Treasury Bills)
(1) Notices to registered holders of registered treasury bills shall be sent to the addresses registered in the electronic treasury bill register. <Amended 2016.9.2>
(2) Notices to treasury bill holders may be replaced by public announcement.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 70 (Forms for Registration Requests, etc.) The Governor of the Bank of Korea shall prescribe forms concerning registration, change, cancellation and notification of registration, etc. of treasury bills.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

SECTION 4 INVESTMENT OF NATIONAL FUNDS <Amended 2005.6.30>

Article 71 (Entrustment of Trading in Government and Public Bonds, etc.)
(1) When buying or selling government and public bonds and monetary stabilization bonds under the Bank of Korea Monetary Stabilization Bond Act (hereinafter referred to as “government and public bonds, etc.”) under Article 34(1)1 or (3) of the Act, the Minister of Finance and Economy or the head of a central government agency may entrust such trading to a person designated by that Minister. A fee not exceeding 10/1,000 of the purchase or sale proceeds may be paid. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) “Financial companies, etc. prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 34(1)2 of the Act means banks, investment traders, investment brokers and securities finance companies under the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act (hereinafter referred to as “national funds investment financial companies”).
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 72 (Trading Prices of Government and Public Bonds, etc.)
(1) Where the Minister of Finance and Economy or the head of a central government agency trades government and public bonds, etc. (including repurchase transactions) to invest national funds outside the securities market under Article 176-9(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act, the trading price shall be determined by any of the following methods: <Amended 2013.8.27, 2025.12.30>
1. For government and public bonds, etc. issued at face value, the amount including interest equivalent calculated daily for the period from issuance to the trading date at the interest rate shall be the purchase and sale price; for those issued at a discount, the yield shall instead be applied. The time of payment of the interest equivalent may be separately determined;
2. In cases other than subparagraph 1, the purchase price shall be the amount assessed by the method determined by the Minister of Finance and Economy using the yield for the government and public bonds, etc. announced by the Korea Exchange on the day before purchase; the sale price shall be the purchase price plus interest calculated daily from purchase to sale at the yield applied at purchase;
3. For purchases subject to repurchase, in addition to the methods in subparagraphs 1 and 2, the issue price of the government and public bonds, etc. may be the purchase price, and that price plus interest calculated daily from purchase to sale at the interest rate determined by the Minister of Finance and Economy considering market interest rates formed in the money market may be the sale price.
(2) Interest paid during the repurchase period on government and public bonds, etc. purchased subject to repurchase shall be paid to the repurchaser and settled upon repurchase.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 73 (Interest Rates for Deposits or Loans) Where the Minister of Finance and Economy or the head of a central government agency deposits or lends national funds under Article 34(1)2 or (3) of the Act, the interest rate shall be determined considering rates applied by the Government or national funds investment financial companies to similar transactions: Provided, That a separate rate may apply where necessary to promote issuance and circulation of State bonds. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 74 (Security) Where necessary when investing national funds, the Minister of Finance and Economy or the head of a central government agency may have the Bank of Korea acquire as security State bonds, payment guarantees of national funds investment financial companies, or listed securities determined by the Minister of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 75 (Investment of Surplus Cash of Funds) When investing surplus cash of a fund under Article 34(4) of the Act, the head of a central government agency shall apply relevant statutes or regulations; where those statutes or regulations do not separately provide otherwise, the cash may be invested by the methods under Article 34(1) of the Act and Articles 71 through 74 of this Decree.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 76 (Accounting Institutions for the National Funds Investment Account)
(1) The Minister of Finance and Economy and the head of a central government agency shall appoint, from among subordinate public officials, an officer authorizing transactions in the national funds investment account (hereinafter referred to in this Section as the “account transaction authorizing officer”) to perform affairs concerning collection of receipts and commitments to pay arising from operation of the national funds investment account under Article 34(6) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the “investment account”), and a cashier for the national funds investment account (hereinafter referred to in this Section as the “account cashier”) to perform affairs concerning receipt and payment of cash and securities arising from operation of that account. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) Appointment of the account transaction authorizing officer and account cashier under paragraph (1) may be replaced by designation of positions established in the relevant agency.
(3) Upon appointing an account transaction authorizing officer, the Minister of Finance and Economy shall notify the Board of Audit and Inspection; upon appointing an account cashier, the Minister shall notify both the Board of Audit and Inspection and the Bank of Korea. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 77 (Accounting Divisions of the Investment Account) The Minister of Finance and Economy shall account separately for the investment account as the national funds deposit account (hereinafter referred to as the “deposit account”), the national funds detailed investment account (hereinafter referred to as the “detailed investment account”) and the national funds investment income account (hereinafter referred to as the “income account”). <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 78 (Receipts and Disbursements of the Deposit Account, etc.)
(1) The deposit account shall record as receipts and disbursements the cash deposited and returned between respective accounts and subaccounts.
(2) The detailed investment account shall record as receipts cash deposited in the investment account from respective accounts and subaccounts for investment of national funds and amounts recovered from investments; it shall record as disbursements cash refunding deposits received from respective accounts and subaccounts and cash paid for investment by the methods in the subparagraphs of Article 34(1) of the Act.
(3) The income account shall record as receipts investment income from invested cash under Article 34(1) of the Act, interest received on deposited cash under Article 51(3) of this Decree, and principal and interest on invested cash under paragraph (4) of this Article; it shall record as disbursements cash paid for the purposes under Article 34(2) of the Act and interest paid on deposited cash under Article 51(3) of this Decree.
(4) The account transaction authorizing officer may invest cash of the income account under Article 34(1) of the Act to the extent that transactions in the income account are not impeded.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 79 (Payment Notices for Receipts of the Investment Account) An account transaction authorizing officer intending to collect receipts of the investment account shall give a payment notice to the person liable to pay: Provided, That notice may be omitted where considered unnecessary.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 80 (Payments from the Investment Account)
(1) An account transaction authorizing officer intending to make a payment from the investment account shall forward documents concerning the commitment to pay to the account cashier.
(2) When making payment, the account cashier shall send a national funds deposit request, etc. to the Bank of Korea and pay by transfer into the creditor's account.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 81 (Maintenance of Books and Records)
(1) An account transaction authorizing officer shall maintain a receipts collection ledger, a commitment-to-pay ledger and subsidiary books, and record necessary matters.
(2) An account cashier shall maintain a receipts ledger, a payments ledger, books concerning securities and subsidiary books, and record necessary matters.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 82 (Maintenance and Preparation of Registers) The Minister of Finance and Economy shall maintain the following registers and record necessary matters concerning cash raised or invested in connection with them: <Amended 2025.12.30>
1. Treasury bill register;
2. Bank of Korea borrowing register;
3. National funds deposit register;
4. National funds detailed investment register;
5. National funds investment income register.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 83 (Notification to the Board of Audit and Inspection) The Minister of Finance and Economy shall notify the Board of Audit and Inspection of the quarterly accounting status of the investment account by the last day of the month following the quarter's final month: Provided, That notification for the fourth quarter shall be replaced by a comprehensive report for the relevant fiscal year. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 84 (Fees for Handling Affairs) The Minister of Finance and Economy may pay fees within actual costs for affairs entrusted to and handled by the Governor of the Bank of Korea under Article 44 of the Act. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

CHAPTER VII HANDLING OF NATIONAL FUNDS BY THE BANK OF KOREA, ETC.

Article 85 (Deposit of National Funds)
(1) Except as otherwise provided by statutes or regulations, cash owned by the State shall be deposited with the Bank of Korea as determined by the Minister of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), cash belonging to an enterprise special account or a fund may be placed with a treasury bank as determined by the Minister of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 86 (Transactions in National Funds by the Bank of Korea, etc.)
(1) The Bank of Korea, etc. shall handle transactions in national funds as prescribed by this Decree and Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) National funds received by the Bank of Korea, etc. shall constitute State deposits; matters necessary for the types and receipt and payment of deposits shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(3) The Bank of Korea shall pay interest on State deposits under paragraph (2) as determined by the Minister of Finance and Economy in consultation with the Governor of the Bank of Korea. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(4) A treasury bank shall pay interest on State deposits placed with it as agreed between the head of the central government agency and that bank, considering interest rates applied to similar transactions.
(5) Financial companies, etc. shall pay interest on treasury deposits placed with them at a rate corresponding to that of ordinary deposits they handle.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 87 (Duty of the Bank of Korea, etc. to Prepare Reports, etc.)
(1) In connection with handling national funds, the Bank of Korea, etc. shall prepare and submit to the Minister of Finance and Economy the reports prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy, such as a statement of financial position for national funds. <Amended 2021.1.5, 2025.12.30>
(2) The Bank of Korea, etc. shall prepare and submit statements of accounts concerning national funds management to the Board of Audit and Inspection as separately prescribed by that Board.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

CHAPTER VIII CASHIERS

Article 88 (Duties of Cashiers) Cashiers (including public officials acting in all of a cashier's affairs or separately undertaking part of them under Article 40(2) of the Act; hereinafter the same applies) shall receive, pay and hold cash as prescribed by this Decree and Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 89 (Inspection of Cashiers' Books and Safes)
(1) The head of a central government agency or a public official delegated by that head shall appoint an inspecting official to conduct periodic or ad hoc inspections of a cashier's books and cash on December 31 each year and upon a cashier's transfer, dismissal or other change: Provided, That periodic inspections of the books and safe of a cashier who has received cash in advance to meet temporary disbursements may be omitted.
(2) Where necessary, the Minister of Finance and Economy or the head of a central government agency may appoint an inspecting official at any time to inspect a cashier's books and cash. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(3) The relevant cashier shall be required to participate in an inspection under paragraphs (1) and (2): Provided, That if unavoidable reasons prevent that cashier's participation, the cashier's acting officer or a public official designated by the head of the central government agency or a public official delegated by that head shall participate.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 90 (Inspection of Other Public Money) Where a cashier concurrently manages transactions in other public money, the inspecting official under Article 89 shall also inspect that other public money.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 91 (Inspection Reports)
(1) Upon inspecting a cashier's books and safe, the inspecting official under Article 89 shall prepare two copies of the inspection report, deliver one to the cashier or the person who participated in place of the cashier under the proviso to Article 89(3) (hereinafter referred to as the “cashier, etc.”), and submit the other to the head of the competent central government agency: Provided, That where the Minister of Finance and Economy appointed the inspecting official under Article 89(2), an inspection report shall also be submitted to that Minister. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) The inspecting official and the relevant cashier, etc. shall affix their names and seals to the inspection report under paragraph (1).
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

CHAPTER IX PREPARATION AND SUBMISSION OF BOOKS AND REPORTS, ETC.

SECTION 1 PREPARATION OF BOOKS

Article 92 (Books to Be Maintained by Public Officials Engaged in Accounting, etc.)
(1) Revenue collection officers shall maintain a receipts collection ledger and record matters concerning collection of receipts.
(2) Finance officers shall maintain a commitment-to-disburse ledger and record matters concerning commitments to disburse.
(3) Disbursing officers shall maintain a disbursements ledger and record matters concerning disbursements.
(4) Cashiers shall maintain a cash transactions ledger and record matters concerning cash transactions.
(5) Officers authorizing transactions in funds for advance use shall maintain a disbursements ledger in the same manner as disbursing officers and record matters concerning payment orders.
(6) The books under paragraphs (1) through (4) shall be closed as at the last day of each month.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 93 (Maintenance of Revenue and Expenditure Ledgers, etc.)
(1) The head of a central government agency shall maintain a revenue ledger and an expenditure ledger and record details of receipts and disbursements of accounts or subaccounts under that agency's jurisdiction: Provided, That for funds, a fund receipts ledger and a fund disbursements ledger shall be maintained to record details of fund receipts and disbursements.
(2) Books prepared by the head of a central government agency under paragraph (1) may be replaced by aggregated data from reports submitted through the digital budget and accounting system by subordinate public officials engaged in accounting under Article 97(1) and (2) and Article 98(3).
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 94 (Preparation and Maintenance of General Revenue and Expenditure Ledgers)
(1) The Minister of Finance and Economy shall maintain a general revenue ledger and a general expenditure ledger to record details of State revenues and expenditures. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) The general revenue and expenditure ledgers under paragraph (1) may be replaced by aggregated data from reports submitted by the heads of central government agencies through the digital budget and accounting system under Article 97(1) and (2) and Article 98(3).
(3) On February 10 of each fiscal year, the Minister of Finance and Economy shall close the general revenue and expenditure ledgers for the immediately preceding fiscal year in the presence of an Audit Commissioner and other public officials designated by the Chairperson of the Board of Audit and Inspection. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 95 (Methods of Recording Books and Forms)
(1) Books under Article 92 shall be recorded without delay whenever a cause for an entry arises.
(2) Matters concerning forms of books under Articles 92 through 94 shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 96 (Books to Be Maintained by the Bank of Korea, etc.) The Bank of Korea, etc. shall maintain books for recording transactions in national funds as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy, and record transactions in cash handled for the State. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

SECTION 2 SUBMISSION OF REPORTS, ETC.

Article 97 (Reporting of Collection Performance, etc.)
(1) Revenue collection officers, finance officers and disbursing officers (hereinafter referred to in this Article as “revenue collection officers, etc.”) shall respectively prepare monthly reports on collection of receipts, amounts committed to disburse and disbursed amounts and submit them to the head of the central government agency to which they belong by the fifth of the following month; that head shall compile and submit them to the Minister of Finance and Economy by the seventh of the following month. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) Where revenue collection officers, etc. conduct cash transactions during the cash transactions closing period, notwithstanding paragraph (1) and Article 92(6), they shall close the books as at the final day of that period and submit revised final reports to the head of the central government agency by January 25; that head shall compile and submit them to the Minister of Finance and Economy by January 31. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(3) Cashiers shall prepare cash transaction reports and report to revenue collection officers and disbursing officers by the third of the following month.
(4) At the request of the head of a central government agency, the Minister of Finance and Economy may extend the deadline for reports submitted by that head under paragraph (1). <Amended 2025.12.30>
(5) Forms for the reports under paragraphs (1) and (3) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(6) The Minister of Finance and Economy shall prescribe the contents and preparation guidelines for reports under paragraphs (1) through (3). <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 98 (Preparation and Submission of Books and Reports through the Digital Budget and Accounting System)
(1) Revenue collection officers, finance officers, disbursing officers, cashiers, heads of central government agencies and the Minister of Finance and Economy shall record and maintain books under Articles 92 and 94 using the digital budget and accounting system. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) The Minister of Finance and Economy shall record and maintain the registers in the subparagraphs of Article 82 using the digital budget and accounting system. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(3) Reports under Article 97(1) through (3) shall be submitted by transmission through the digital budget and accounting system: Provided, That where an unavoidable reason prevents use of that system, reports may be submitted on information and communications recording media containing the data, such as diskettes or magnetic tapes, or in writing.
(4) Where the head of a central government agency has independently developed and uses information and communications media and programs for financial operation, notwithstanding the main clause of paragraph (3), the head may submit reports using those media and programs after prior consultation with the Minister of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(5) When newly developing or supplementing the digital budget and accounting system, the Minister of Finance and Economy shall consult the Board of Audit and Inspection in advance. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 99 (Classification Numbers and Reporting Numbers)
(1) To process reports on national funds management through information and communications media and programs, the Minister of Finance and Economy may separately prescribe necessary classification numbers in addition to budget item classifications under Article 7 of the Enforcement Decree of the National Finance Act. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) To process reports on national funds management through information and communications media and programs, the Minister of Finance and Economy may assign reporting numbers to each revenue collection officer, finance officer and disbursing officer, and may abolish or adjust assigned reporting numbers where necessary. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 100 (Demands for Submission of Reports) Where reports under Article 97 are not submitted within the deadline, the head of the central government agency and the Minister of Finance and Economy shall demand their submission. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 101 (Notification of Changes to Budgets, etc.) The head of a central government agency shall notify the Minister of Finance and Economy when the budget is changed through use of reserve funds, transfers within expenditure budget items, transfers between expenditure budget items, organizational transfers, disbursements in excess of the budget for expenses financed by receipts, disbursements to generate receipts, supplementary revised budgets, etc., or when a fund management plan is modified under Article 70 of the National Finance Act. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 102 (Correction of Errors)
(1) Upon finding an error in the contents of reports and notices received under Articles 97 and 101, the Minister of Finance and Economy shall notify the head of the relevant central government agency and have it corrected. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) The head of a central government agency notified under paragraph (1) shall correct the contents of the reports and notices without delay and take necessary measures.
(3) Where the head of a central government agency finds an error in the contents of reports and notices submitted to the Minister of Finance and Economy, the head shall obtain that Minister's approval to make corrections and take necessary measures. <Amended 2025.12.30>
(4) Revenue collection officers, finance officers and disbursing officers finding errors in reports submitted to the head of a central government agency shall correct them with that head's approval.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 103 (Inspection of Fiscal Statistical Data)
(1) Where necessary for performing national funds management affairs, the Minister of Finance and Economy may inspect, through the digital budget and accounting system, data reported to heads of central government agencies under Article 97(1) and (2). <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) At the request of the Chairperson of the Board of Audit and Inspection, the Minister of Finance and Economy shall permit inspection, through the digital budget and accounting system, of data submitted to the Minister by heads of central government agencies under Article 97(1) and (2). <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 104 (Procedures for Submission of Statements of Accounts) The types of statements of accounts and supporting documents concerning national funds management submitted to the Board of Audit and Inspection by revenue collection officers, finance officers, disbursing officers, officers authorizing transactions in funds for advance use and cashiers under Article 38(4) of the Act, and submission procedures, etc., shall be separately prescribed by that Board.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

CHAPTER X SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 105 (Acting and Divided-Duty Public Officials Engaged in Accounting)
(1) Where the head of a central government agency or a public official delegated by that head has a person act in all the affairs of, or separately undertake part of the affairs of, any person specified below under Article 40 of the Act, the head or delegated official shall notify the persons and institutions specified in the respective subparagraphs:
1. Affairs of a revenue collection officer: The Board of Audit and Inspection and the Bank of Korea, etc.;
2. Affairs of a finance officer: The disbursing officer and the Board of Audit and Inspection;
3. Affairs of a disbursing officer: The finance officer, the Board of Audit and Inspection and the Bank of Korea, etc.;
4. Affairs of an officer authorizing transactions in funds for advance use: The finance officer, the cashier for funds for advance use and the Board of Audit and Inspection;
5. Affairs of a revenue cashier: The revenue collection officer, the Board of Audit and Inspection and the Bank of Korea, etc.;
6. Affairs of an office operating expense cashier: The disbursing officer, the Board of Audit and Inspection and the financial company, etc. with which transactions are conducted;
7. Affairs of a cashier for funds for advance use: The disbursing officer, the officer authorizing transactions in funds for advance use, the Board of Audit and Inspection and the financial company, etc. with which transactions are conducted.
(2) The titles of public officials acting in all affairs of a revenue collection officer, finance officer, disbursing officer, officer authorizing transactions in funds for advance use, revenue cashier, office operating expense cashier or cashier for funds for advance use under Article 40 of the Act (hereinafter referred to in this Article as “acting officers”), public officials separately undertaking part of those affairs (hereinafter referred to in this Article as “divided-duty officers”), and public officials acting for divided-duty officers (hereinafter referred to in this Article as “acting divided-duty officers”) are as set out in the following table.
<img src="http://www.law.go.kr/flDownload.do?flSeq=24990899"  alt="img24990899" >

| Public official engaged in accounting | Title of acting officer | Title of divided-duty officer | Title of acting divided-duty officer |
|---|---|---|---|
| Revenue collection officer | Acting revenue collection officer | Divided-duty revenue collection officer | Acting divided-duty revenue collection officer |
| Finance officer | Acting finance officer | Divided-duty finance officer | Acting divided-duty finance officer |
| Disbursing officer | Acting disbursing officer | Divided-duty disbursing officer | Acting divided-duty disbursing officer |
| Officer authorizing transactions in funds for advance use | Acting officer authorizing transactions in funds for advance use | Divided-duty officer authorizing transactions in funds for advance use | Acting divided-duty officer authorizing transactions in funds for advance use |
| Revenue cashier | Acting revenue cashier | Divided-duty revenue cashier | Acting divided-duty revenue cashier |
| Office operating expense cashier | Acting office operating expense cashier | Divided-duty office operating expense cashier | Acting divided-duty office operating expense cashier |
| Cashier for funds for advance use | Acting cashier for funds for advance use | Divided-duty cashier for funds for advance use | Acting divided-duty cashier for funds for advance use |

</img>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 106 (Status Reports on Public Officials Engaged in Accounting) The head of a central government agency shall prepare the status of public officials engaged in accounting specified in Article 105(1) and acting and divided-duty public officials engaged in accounting under Article 105(2) as at the last day of each fiscal year, classified by accounting position and by Special Metropolitan City, Metropolitan City, Special Self-Governing City, Province and Special Self-Governing Province, and submit it to the Minister of Finance and Economy by January 31 of the following fiscal year. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 107 (Appointment of Public Officials of Other Central Government Agencies to Accounting Positions)
(1) In any of the following cases, the head of a central government agency shall obtain prior consent from the head of the other central government agency concerning the official to receive delegation and the scope of affairs to be delegated:
1. Intending to delegate revenue collection officer or finance officer affairs to a public official of another central government agency under Article 41 of the Act, or to delegate to such a public official the role of acting in all or separately undertaking part of those affairs under Article 40 of the Act;
2. Intending to appoint a public official of another central government agency as a disbursing officer or cashier under Article 41 of the Act, or to appoint such a public official to act in all or separately undertake part of those affairs under Article 40 of the Act.
(2) Upon delegating accounting affairs to a public official of another central government agency or appointing such a public official to an accounting position under paragraph (1), the head of a central government agency shall notify the Minister of Finance and Economy and the Board of Audit and Inspection. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 108 (Handling of National Funds Management Affairs by Local Government Public Officials)
(1) The head of a central government agency intending to have local government public officials or executives and employees of public institutions under the Act on the Management of Public Institutions (hereinafter referred to as “public institutions”) or financial companies, etc. handle national funds management affairs under Article 42(1) of the Act shall obtain prior consent from the head of the relevant local government, public institution or financial company, etc.
(2) Upon having local government public officials or executives and employees of public institutions or financial companies, etc. handle national funds management affairs under paragraph (1), the head of a central government agency shall notify the Minister of Finance and Economy and the Board of Audit and Inspection. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 109 (Fidelity Guarantees for Public Officials Engaged in Accounting)
(1) The head of a central government agency shall determine and administer matters concerning fidelity guarantees for subordinate public officials engaged in accounting under Article 45 of the Act.
(2) The Minister of Finance and Economy shall prescribe common matters necessary for fidelity guarantees under paragraph (1). <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 109-2 (Rounding of National Funds)
(1) “Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the proviso to Article 47(1) of the Act means cases of installment collection, acceptance or payment of national funds in which the installment amount or fractional amount is added to the first receipt or payment because either of the following applies:
1. The installment amount is less than 10 won;
2. The installment amount includes a fraction of less than 10 won.
(2) “Public institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the Korea Development Bank under the Korea Development Bank Act” in the proviso to Article 47(3) of the Act means the following public institutions:
1. Korea Development Bank;
2. Industrial Bank of Korea.
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

Article 109-3 (Processing of Unique Identifying Information) The heads of central government agencies and financial companies, etc. may process data containing resident registration numbers under Article 19, subparagraph 1, of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act where unavoidable for performing the following affairs:
1. Receipt, payment and custody of cash under Article 4-3 of the Act;
2. Collection of receipts under Article 10 of the Act;
3. Electronic service of payment notices under Article 11 of the Act;
4. Return of erroneous payments and overpayments under Article 15 of the Act;
5. Refund of receipts under Article 16 of the Act;
6. Transactions in funds for advance use under Article 18 of the Act;
7. Disbursements under Article 22 of the Act;
8. Payment of office operating expenses under Article 24 of the Act;
9. Return of disbursed funds under Article 29 of the Act.
[Wholly amended 2014.8.6]

Article 110 (Matters concerning National Funds Management) The Minister of Finance and Economy may determine matters necessary for national funds management in addition to those prescribed in this Decree. <Amended 2025.12.30>
[Wholly amended 2012.12.27]

